    "faq": {
      "type": "rv-faq",
      "blocks": {
        "q_1": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What is an RV deck kit?",
            "answer": "<p>An RV deck kit is a complete, ready-to-assemble deck that you build beside your camper at a seasonal or long-stay site. An A-DIY RV deck kit includes the engineered lumber, Tuff Block footings, galvanized hardware, a custom drill bit and step-by-step instructions, so a power drill is the only tool you need.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q_2": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What's the best deck for an RV trailer?",
            "answer": "<p>For a seasonal or long-stay site, the best deck for an RV trailer is a freestanding deck on its own footings, sized to the door side of your rig. Portable fold-out platforms suit travelers who change campsites every few days.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q_3": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Does an RV deck attach to the camper?",
            "answer": "<p>No. An A-DIY deck is freestanding. It stands on its own footings beside your RV and is never fastened to it, so your RV stays free to move.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q_4": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What size deck is best for a travel trailer or camper?",
            "answer": "<p>Most travel trailers suit a long, narrow deck along the door side, such as 6x16, 8x16 or 8x20. Measure the open strip between your RV and the edge of your site, then check your campground's size limit. Wider lots can fit a 12x12 or larger standard deck.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q_5": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How do you build a deck for a camper?",
            "answer": "<p>Check your campground's rules, choose a size, level the pad, set the included Tuff Block footings, assemble the frame and screw down the boards. A power drill is the only tool you need, and most owners finish in 8–16 hours.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q_6": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Do I need my campground's permission to put a deck on my site?",
            "answer": "<p>Usually, yes. Many seasonal parks set their own deck rules for size, height and placement, and ask you to have your plan approved before you build. Check with your park office first. Engineering diagrams are available for every A-DIY kit to support your request.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q_7": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Do I need a building permit for an RV deck?",
            "answer": "<p>It depends on your town or county. Permit requirements vary with deck height, size and placement, so check with your local building department as well as your campground. A-DIY engineering diagrams are available to support a permit application.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q_8": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How high off the ground is an RV deck?",
            "answer": "<p>Each A-DIY kit assembles at 15–17 inches off the ground. Longer post kits with bracing raise it, and digging a few inches where the footings sit lowers it. Steps can bridge the gap between your RV door and the deck.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q_9": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Is an A-DIY RV deck portable?",
            "answer": "<p>Not in the fold-up sense. An A-DIY deck is screwed together on footings rather than set in concrete, and it is built to last for years, not to fold up between trips. If you plan to move sites, talk to our team before you order about taking your deck apart and rebuilding it.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q_10": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How much weight can an A-DIY RV deck hold?",
            "answer": "<p>Every A-DIY deck system is engineered for 15,000+ lbs. The frame uses 6x6 beams and 2x8 joists spaced 13 to 14 inches on center, with galvanized, corrosion-resistant hardware throughout.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q_11": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Can I build an RV deck on uneven ground?",
            "answer": "<p>Yes, with a little site prep. Your deck sits on Tuff Block footings on a prepared, level pad, so you level the ground where the footings go before you start. If your site has a noticeable slope, our team will recommend the right setup before you order.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q_12": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Can I add steps or railings to my RV deck?",
            "answer": "<p>Yes. Steps and railings can be added to your A-DIY kit and matched to your deck height. Ask our team through chat or during your free design consultation.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q_13": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Can I put a roof or gazebo over my camper deck?",
            "answer": "<p>Yes. A freestanding gazebo or pergola can sit over your deck for shade and shelter. Check your campground's rules on covered structures, and talk to our team about the size and layout before you order.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q_14": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How is an RV deck kit delivered to a campground?",
            "answer": "<p>Kits ship by LTL ground freight within the continental US, typically in 10–14 business days, with curbside delivery scheduled by the carrier. Let your park office know a freight delivery is coming, and tell our team if a truck can't reach your site.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q_15": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How much does an RV deck kit cost?",
            "answer": "<p>A-DIY RV deck kits start at [wood_from] for a [wood_from_size] pressure-treated wood deck. Composite kits start at [composite_from] for a [composite_from_size]. Every kit includes the lumber, footings and hardware. Financing and a $250 A-DIY Reserve deposit are available.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q_16": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Where are A-DIY deck kits made?",
            "answer": "<p>Every A-DIY component is sourced and manufactured in the USA. A-DIY is a registered trademark of Laccoo Outdoors, LLC, based in Piscataway, New Jersey.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
